<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diarmait Mac Murchada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1110–<a href="/years/1171">1171</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Dermot MacMurrough</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diarmait Mac Murchada was King of Leinster of the Uí Chennselaig dynasty. Driven out by the High King Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigernan-ua-ruairc">Tigernán Ua Ruairc</a> in 1166, he sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and recruited Anglo-Norman knights, above all Richard de Clare, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, who married his daughter Aoife. Their capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1170 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada.jpg" alt="Diarmait Mac Murchada" width="262" height="720" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giraldus Cambrensis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diarmaid_Mac_Murrough_Expugnatio_Hibernica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diarmait was a son of Donnchadh mac Murchada, King of Leinster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, who was killed in 1115 fighting Domnall Gerrlámhach, King of Dublin, and buried by the citizens with the carcass of a dog. He became king unexpectedly after the death of his older brother Énna. The High King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toirdelbach-ua-conchobair">Toirdelbach Ua Conchobair</a>, fearing a rival, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigernan-ua-ruairc">Tigernán Ua Ruairc</a> to oust him; Ua Ruairc slaughtered the livestock of Leinster, but Diarmait regained his throne with the help of the Leinster clans in 1132. His reputed abduction of Ua Ruairc's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbforgaill">Derbforgaill</a>, whom other sources describe as a willing guest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a>, deepened the enmity between the two kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Between 1140 and 1170 Diarmait commissioned Irish Romanesque buildings at Baltinglass Abbey, St Saviour's Priory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glendalough">Glendalough</a>, Ferns Abbey and Killeshin Church, and founded convents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, at Aghade and at Kilculliheen. He married Mór, half-sister of St Lawrence O'Toole, in 1153 and presided at the synod of Clane in 1161 where O'Toole was installed as archbishop of Dublin.</p><p>After his exile, Henry II allowed him to recruit soldiers in his realms, among them Robert FitzStephen and Maurice FitzGerald, who were promised lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a>. They landed at Bannow Bay and took Wexford in May 1169, then raided Ossory. Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair negotiated with him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a>, letting him keep Leinster in return for recognising Ruaidrí as High King; some historians hold that he secretly promised to bring in no more foreigners. After FitzGerald's arrival in 1170, Diarmait marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, which surrendered, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>'s marriage to Aoife followed the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Grief-stricken at the death of his youngest son, Conchobar, a hostage of the High King, Diarmait retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a> and died about 1 May 1171; his reputed grave is in the cathedral graveyard there. Henry II's larger invasion that year created the Lordship of Ireland. Remembered as Diarmait na nGall, Diarmait of the Foreigners, he was often called a traitor in histories written after 1800, though his aim was to become High King himself. Gerald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, whose kinsmen fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, described him as a man who preferred to be feared by all rather than loved by any.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diarmait Mac Murchada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tigernan Ua Ruairc, ruler from about 1124 to 1172, fought <b>Diarmait Mac Murchada</b> of Leinster, who had abducted his wife Derbforgaill; their feud helped bring the Normans to Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-breifne">Kingdom of Breifne</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1170 Strongbow and <b>Diarmait Mac Murchada</b> captured it, and in 1171 Henry II made it the capital of the Lordship of Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dublin">Kingdom of Dublin</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1166 <b>Diarmait Mac Murchada</b> was expelled and sought help from Henry II.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-leinster">Kingdom of Leinster</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1170 Strongbow and Raymond le Gros stormed the town, and Strongbow married Aoife, daughter of <b>Diarmait Mac Murchada</b>, there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-waterford">Kingdom of Waterford</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Diarmait Mac Murchada</b>, deposed king of Leinster, sought Norman help and promised his daughter Aoife and succession to Richard de Clare, Strongbow.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leinster">Lordship of Leinster</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Diarmait Mac Murchada</b>, driven out in 1166, brought Anglo-Norman knights to Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/state/ui-chennselaig">Ui Chennselaig</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Leinster, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1149" aria-label="Leinster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4411 329 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Diarmait Mac Murchada"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1149">What was here in 1149? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
